# OmniOPSD training code bundle

This bundle contains the code needed to build and run the four comparable
Qwen2.5-Omni LoRA arms: SFT, GRPO, standard answer-privileged OPSD, and
CLUE-OPSD. It also keeps the older VideoOdyssey matrix/evaluation entry points
because they use the same arm launcher. Dataset files, raw videos, model
weights, checkpoints, logs, and Python environments are intentionally absent.

## What is in the bundle

- `src/omni_opsd/`: data contracts, matrix row construction, MCQ rewards,
  evaluation, and OPSD/GKD utility code.
- `scripts/build_omnivideo_training_matrix.py`: turns a canonical
  OmniVideo-100K manifest into four answer-isolated JSONL arms with identical
  full-video student inputs.
- `scripts/run_training_arm_a100.sh`: CUDA/A100 entry point for one arm.
- `scripts/run_omnivideo_baseline_queue.sh`: smoke then full runs for a list
  of arms on one host; its monitor supports both `nvidia-smi` and `npu-smi`.
- `scripts/run_video_odyssey_training_arm.sh` and related builders/evaluators:
  the VideoOdyssey-compatible path used by the same four arms.
- `patches/`: bounded A/V compatibility patches and the generic rollout
  protocol patch. The exact ms-swift feature commit is recorded in
  `third_party_ms_swift_commit.txt`.

## Environment on an A100 host

Install a CUDA-compatible PyTorch build first, then install this package and
the training-only dependencies. Do not install an Ascend/torch_npu wheel on
the A100 host.

```bash
python -m venv .venv
source .venv/bin/activate
python -m pip install -U pip
python -m pip install -e '.[qwen25-training,test]'
python -m pip install -r requirements-training.txt
```

`ms-swift` is kept as an external checkout because it is a separate project:

```bash
git clone https://github.com/modelscope/ms-swift.git /data/ms-swift
git -C /data/ms-swift checkout 960c5bf2cb070d1e3483ed93965f2e338d3ae93a
git -C /data/ms-swift apply /path/to/OmniOPSD/patches/ms-swift-infer-protocol.patch
```

The pinned checkout already contains the `teacher_videos` and
`clue_ema_alpha` support used by OPSD/CLUE-OPSD. The two local
`ms-swift-qwen25-bounded-*.patch` files are historical fallbacks for a clean
checkout before the pinned feature commits; applying them again to the pinned
checkout is unnecessary and may report an already-applied patch.

## Build the four OmniVideo arms

The canonical JSONL must contain the selected rows and local video paths. Each
row needs `sample_id`, `video_id`, `video_path`, `duration`, `question`, four
`choices`, `answer`, and non-empty `evidence_spans`. The builder checks all
media paths and enforces the fixed F1 contract.

```bash
export PYTHONPATH="$PWD/src"
python scripts/build_omnivideo_training_matrix.py \
  --canonical /data/omnivideo/gap5000.canonical.jsonl \
  --output-dir /data/omnivideo/training_matrix \
  --fps 2 --max-frames 768 --min-pixels 3136 --max-pixels 28672 \
  --use-audio-in-video
```

The output directory contains `omnivideo_100k_train.{sft,grpo,opsd,clue_opsd}.jsonl`
and `training_matrix_summary.json`. The student side is the same full video
and prompt in all four files. SFT puts the answer in the assistant target;
GRPO puts it only in `solution`; OPSD gives it only to the full-video teacher;
CLUE-OPSD gives the teacher evidence-interval videos without the answer.

## Run one arm on CUDA/A100

Set the data, model, ms-swift, and Python dependency paths, then choose the
number of visible GPUs. The wrapper mirrors `CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES` into the
legacy device-count check; PyTorch still uses CUDA normally.

```bash
export CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0,1,2,3
export OMNI_OPSD_MODEL=/data/models/Qwen2.5-Omni-7B
export OMNI_OPSD_MS_SWIFT_ROOT=/data/ms-swift
export OMNI_OPSD_PYTHON_DEPS="$PWD"       # or a site-packages directory
export OMNI_OPSD_DATASET=/data/omnivideo/training_matrix/omnivideo_100k_train.sft.jsonl
export OMNI_OPSD_OUTPUT_DIR=/data/runs/omnivideo_sft
export OMNI_OPSD_MAX_STEPS=300
export OMNI_OPSD_ATTN_IMPL=sdpa
export OMNI_OPSD_GKD_SAFE_MODE=0          # normal CUDA gradient clipping
export USE_AUDIO_IN_VIDEO=1

bash scripts/run_training_arm_a100.sh sft
```

For the other arms, change the positional arm, dataset suffix, and output
directory to `grpo`, `opsd`, or `clue_opsd`. A one-step run is recommended
before a long run (`OMNI_OPSD_MAX_STEPS=1`). `opsd` and `clue_opsd` use the
LoRA-shadow EMA teacher mode exposed by the pinned ms-swift commit; this is a
LoRA implementation and is recorded as `paper_exact=false` by the launcher.

On an eight-GPU A100 host, the queue can run all four arms sequentially after
the matrix is built:

```bash
export CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0,1,2,3,4,5,6,7
export ASCEND_RT_VISIBLE_DEVICES="$CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES"  # count check only
export OMNI_OPSD_PROJECT_ROOT="$PWD"
export OMNI_OPSD_QUEUE_ROOT=/data/runs/omnivideo_queue
export OMNI_OPSD_MATRIX_DATASET_ROOT=/data/omnivideo/training_matrix
export OMNI_OPSD_MODEL=/data/models/Qwen2.5-Omni-7B
export OMNI_OPSD_MS_SWIFT_ROOT=/data/ms-swift
export OMNI_OPSD_PYTHON_DEPS="$PWD"
export OMNI_OPSD_QUEUE_ARMS=sft,grpo,opsd,clue_opsd
bash scripts/run_omnivideo_baseline_queue.sh
```

The queue first performs one-step smoke runs and skips a full arm if its smoke
fails. It writes a per-arm `RUN_CLASSIFICATION.txt`, logs, and completion
markers under `OMNI_OPSD_QUEUE_ROOT`.

## Checks before handing the bundle to another machine

```bash
PYTHONPATH=.:src pytest -q \
  tests/test_swift_opsd_dataset.py \
  tests/test_rewards.py \
  tests/test_evaluation.py \
  tests/test_materialize_local_training_matrix.py
bash -n scripts/run_training_arm_a100.sh scripts/run_omnivideo_baseline_queue.sh
```

The training code never bundles or downloads model/media assets. Copy the
canonical matrix inputs and raw videos to the A100 host separately, and keep
their checksums with the run metadata.
